cartridge "LARC-I MISSION/J.BEDARD/1986"

' LARC-I MISSION
' A ColecoVision game based on "The LARC-I Mission" by Joseph Bedard,
' Ahoy! September 1986 (Commodore 64 BASIC with a machine-code joystick /
' missile interrupt and a machine-code scroller in DATA, disk
' ahoy1986-09, "LARC-I"; the author's name is in the listing's REMs and on
' its title screen). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from the
' listing and its interrupt routine, orig/larc-i.txt), the game is built
' for the ColecoVision: a green plain that scrolls down one pixel at a
' time under a white delta jet and its shadow, grey rock ridges, radar
' masts, pines, bunker domes and craters, a red heat seeker, sounds.
'
' The game: you fly the LARC-I low over enemy land. The ground scrolls
' towards you; rock ridges cross the whole land with two gaps, and masts,
' pines and domes stand in your way. Touching any of them destroys the
' jet. A heat-seeking missile climbs from the bottom of the screen right
' under the jet, always following it across: the only cover is to keep
' something solid between you and it, so it bursts on the ground instead.
' If it reaches the jet, the mission ends. Every row of land flown is 5
' (distance); the longest distance is kept.
'
' From the original (orig/): the land scrolls down a row at a time; each
' row a random object (one of three, or none) comes in at a random column,
' a row is left empty after each, and every L1 rows (31 EASY, 21 MODERATE,
' 11 DIFFICULT) a two-row ridge crosses the screen with two 4-character
' gaps at random places; distance + 5 a row; the jet moves with the stick
' inside the lower half of the screen; the heat seeker starts below the
' screen, rises 1 pixel a frame and keeps the jet's x every frame; it
' bursts on any background character and a new one starts at once; it
' kills when it reaches the jet's height; touching the background kills;
' the scroll is quicker at a higher level (the BASIC delay loop L2); a
' single jet; GAME OVER, then CONGRATULATIONS! / LONGEST DISTANCE YET for
' a new record; LEVEL 1-3 chosen on the title.
'
' Liberties: the land scrolls smoothly (1/2, 5/8 and 3/4 pixel a frame
' for the three levels) instead of 8 pixels a step; it is built of lines
' 24 pixels apart, each a ridge, one or two objects or a crater (the C64
' put at most one object in a row); ridges every 12, 9 or 6 lines (the
' C64's 31, 21 and 11 rows were too tight at these speeds); objects are
' 16 pixels wide and collide by boxes drawn inside their shapes; the jet
' moves 2 pixels a frame across and 1 up and down, diagonals too (the C64
' read only the four straight directions); the heat seeker starts at the
' bottom of the visible field, climbs 11/16, 12/16 or 13/16 pixel a frame
' (the C64: 1) and waits 90, 70 or 55 frames after a burst before the
' next launch (the C64 relaunched at once); a puff shows where it burst;
' the field starts full of land (none near the jet) and the first launch
' comes after 2 1/2 seconds; the jet's sprite collisions became boxes;
' the three screen thirds share one pattern table (VDP register 4 = 0) so
' the scrolling codes are rewritten once; GAME OVER, then
' the title (the C64 went straight back to its level menu); a joystick
' menu instead of the keys 1-3; title tune, credits page, the best shown
' on the title and during play.
'
' Standing still cannot last: the land under the jet scrolls away and the
' heat seeker gets through as soon as nothing is below it. Sweeping from
' side to side runs into masts and ridges.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick flies the jet. On the title,
' up and down choose a level (or the credits), a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects
' after; the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always
' given. Own xorshift random numbers.
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs larc-call-div-in-sum removed 2026-11-01.

